window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES["ph-career"] = {
 "Welcome to the Course": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Welcome to the Course</strong><br><span>Your roadmap to a public health career</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Welcome to the Course\"><figcaption>Welcome to the Course: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explore</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Welcome to the Course.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Assess</strong><span>Apply Welcome to the Course to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plan</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Welcome to the Course</summary><p>Welcome to the Course is the focus of this Launch Your Public Health Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to understand public-health career opportunities and build a realistic career plan.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>career pathway</summary><p>A sequence of education, experience, and roles leading toward a professional goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transferable skill</summary><p>An ability useful across different roles and sectors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SMART goal</summary><p>A goal that is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</p></details></section><p>This course prepares public health graduates to launch and advance careers across the United States and other developed economies. Over the coming lessons you will map the job market, explore career pathways, sharpen core technical skills, and build a concrete personal career plan. Whether you aim to join a government agency, a hospital system, academia, or a nonprofit, the material connects classroom theory to real hiring practices. Expect practical tools, worked examples, and actionable steps you can apply immediately.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Scope</strong> – career strategy plus foundational public health science.</li><li><strong>Audience</strong> – recent graduates and early-career professionals.</li><li><strong>Format</strong> – concept lessons paired with how-to strategy modules.</li><li><strong>Outcome</strong> – a written, goal-driven career plan.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A new MPH graduate uses this course to target a fellowship at the CDC and drafts a two-year plan to build the skills that program values.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A structured start turns a degree into a deliberate, competitive career trajectory in a crowded job market.</p></div>",
 "The Public Health Job Market": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 The Public Health Job Market</strong><br><span>Where the jobs are in developed economies</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for The Public Health Job Market\"><figcaption>The Public Health Job Market: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explore</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of The Public Health Job Market.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Assess</strong><span>Apply The Public Health Job Market to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plan</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>The Public Health Job Market</summary><p>The Public Health Job Market is the focus of this Launch Your Public Health Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to understand public-health career opportunities and build a realistic career plan.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>career pathway</summary><p>A sequence of education, experience, and roles leading toward a professional goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transferable skill</summary><p>An ability useful across different roles and sectors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SMART goal</summary><p>A goal that is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</p></details></section><p>The public health workforce in the United States and peer developed nations spans federal, state, and local government, hospitals and health systems, universities, nonprofits, and the pharmaceutical and biotech industry. US Bureau of Labor Statistics data shows epidemiologist and health-services roles growing faster than average. Salaries vary widely: local government analysts may start near 55,000 dollars, while experienced federal scientists and industry epidemiologists earn well into six figures. Understanding sectors helps you target the right entry point.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Government</strong> – CDC, NIH, FDA, HHS, and state and county health departments.</li><li><strong>Health systems</strong> – hospital epidemiology, infection prevention, quality.</li><li><strong>Academia</strong> – research, teaching, grant-funded projects.</li><li><strong>Industry</strong> – pharma, biotech, and health tech.</li><li><strong>Nonprofits</strong> – foundations and advocacy organizations.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An Ohio Department of Health analyst tracks flu trends, then moves to a pharma safety team, roughly doubling salary while using the same core skills.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Knowing sector pay and growth lets you plan strategic moves rather than react to whatever opening appears.</p></div>",
 "Career Pathways in Public Health": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Career Pathways in Public Health</strong><br><span>Many roads from one degree</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Career Pathways in Public Health\"><figcaption>Career Pathways in Public Health: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explore</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Career Pathways in Public Health.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Assess</strong><span>Apply Career Pathways in Public Health to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plan</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Career Pathways in Public Health</summary><p>Career Pathways in Public Health is the focus of this Launch Your Public Health Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to understand public-health career opportunities and build a realistic career plan.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>career pathway</summary><p>A sequence of education, experience, and roles leading toward a professional goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transferable skill</summary><p>An ability useful across different roles and sectors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SMART goal</summary><p>A goal that is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</p></details></section><p>Public health careers rarely follow a single straight line. From a common foundation of population health training, professionals branch into epidemiology, health policy, program management, biostatistics, environmental health, and global health. Pathways cross sectors freely, and many practitioners combine a graduate degree with fellowships, certifications, or doctoral study to advance. Recognizing these routes early helps you choose the experiences and credentials that open the doors you want.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Applied practice</strong> – government surveillance and program roles.</li><li><strong>Research track</strong> – academic and NIH-funded science.</li><li><strong>Policy and management</strong> – leadership and administration.</li><li><strong>Fellowships</strong> – CDC EIS and similar accelerators.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate begins as a county epidemiologist in California, completes the CDC Epidemic Intelligence Service, then leads a policy unit at a state health department.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Seeing pathways as flexible helps you make lateral and upward moves that maximize impact and earnings over a full career.</p></div>",
 "Key Public Health Job Roles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Key Public Health Job Roles</strong><br><span>Who does what in the field</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Key Public Health Job Roles\"><figcaption>Key Public Health Job Roles: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explore</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Key Public Health Job Roles.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Assess</strong><span>Apply Key Public Health Job Roles to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plan</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Key Public Health Job Roles</summary><p>Key Public Health Job Roles is the focus of this Launch Your Public Health Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to understand public-health career opportunities and build a realistic career plan.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>career pathway</summary><p>A sequence of education, experience, and roles leading toward a professional goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transferable skill</summary><p>An ability useful across different roles and sectors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SMART goal</summary><p>A goal that is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</p></details></section><p>Public health is a team enterprise built from distinct but connected roles. Epidemiologists investigate patterns of disease, biostatisticians analyze the data, health educators change behavior, environmental specialists monitor hazards, and program managers keep initiatives on budget and on mission. Each role has a typical entry credential and a defined career ladder. Knowing these titles helps you read job postings accurately and position your resume for the right vacancy.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Epidemiologist</strong> – investigates outbreaks and disease trends.</li><li><strong>Biostatistician</strong> – designs studies and models data.</li><li><strong>Health educator</strong> – plans behavior-change programs.</li><li><strong>Environmental health specialist</strong> – inspects and monitors hazards.</li><li><strong>Program manager</strong> – runs budgets, staff, and timelines.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">During a measles cluster, a California county epidemiologist maps cases, a biostatistician estimates transmission, and a health educator runs a clinic vaccination push.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Matching your strengths to a specific role sharpens your applications and speeds your first hire.</p></div>",
 "Assessing Your Skills and Strengths": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Assessing Your Skills and Strengths</strong><br><span>Know what you bring to the table</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Assessing Your Skills and Strengths\"><figcaption>Assessing Your Skills and Strengths: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explore</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Assessing Your Skills and Strengths.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Assess</strong><span>Apply Assessing Your Skills and Strengths to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plan</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Assessing Your Skills and Strengths</summary><p>Assessing Your Skills and Strengths is the focus of this Launch Your Public Health Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to understand public-health career opportunities and build a realistic career plan.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>career pathway</summary><p>A sequence of education, experience, and roles leading toward a professional goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transferable skill</summary><p>An ability useful across different roles and sectors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SMART goal</summary><p>A goal that is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</p></details></section><p>Before you can target the right job, you need an honest inventory of your abilities. Skills fall into technical categories such as data analysis and study design, and transferable ones such as writing, communication, and project leadership. A structured self-assessment reveals both your strongest selling points and the gaps you must close to reach your target role. Treat this as the foundation of every application you will write.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>List every technical skill you have, from statistical software to survey design.</li><li>Add transferable strengths like writing, presenting, and teamwork.</li><li>Rate each skill honestly as beginner, competent, or advanced.</li><li>Compare your list against three real job postings you admire.</li><li>Mark the gaps and note how you will close each one.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate eyeing an NIH data role finds strong R skills but weak grant writing, so she volunteers on a faculty proposal to build that competency.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>An accurate skills map lets you apply with confidence and invest your learning time where it pays off in the US market.</p></div>",
 "Setting SMART Career Goals": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Setting SMART Career Goals</strong><br><span>Turn ambition into a plan</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Setting SMART Career Goals\"><figcaption>Setting SMART Career Goals: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explore</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Setting SMART Career Goals.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Assess</strong><span>Apply Setting SMART Career Goals to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plan</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Setting SMART Career Goals</summary><p>Setting SMART Career Goals is the focus of this Launch Your Public Health Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to understand public-health career opportunities and build a realistic career plan.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>career pathway</summary><p>A sequence of education, experience, and roles leading toward a professional goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transferable skill</summary><p>An ability useful across different roles and sectors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SMART goal</summary><p>A goal that is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</p></details></section><p>Vague ambitions like wanting a better job rarely produce results. SMART goals are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound, converting wishes into concrete targets you can act on and track. Applied to your career, they clarify exactly what role you want, what evidence proves progress, and by when. Writing goals this way keeps you accountable and makes it obvious when a milestone is reached.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>State the specific role or outcome you want.</li><li>Define a measure that proves you reached it.</li><li>Check that it is achievable given your time and resources.</li><li>Confirm it is relevant to your long-term direction.</li><li>Attach a firm deadline and review date.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate writes: secure an entry epidemiologist post at a state health department within nine months, having completed a SAS certificate and three informational interviews.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Recruiters and mentors respond to focused candidates, and clear goals help you say yes to the right opportunities and no to distractions.</p></div>",
 "Building Your Career Plan": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building Your Career Plan</strong><br><span>From goals to a working document</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-orientation-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Building Your Career Plan\"><figcaption>Building Your Career Plan: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Explore</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Building Your Career Plan.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Assess</strong><span>Apply Building Your Career Plan to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Plan</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Building Your Career Plan</summary><p>Building Your Career Plan is the focus of this Launch Your Public Health Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to understand public-health career opportunities and build a realistic career plan.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>career pathway</summary><p>A sequence of education, experience, and roles leading toward a professional goal.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>transferable skill</summary><p>An ability useful across different roles and sectors.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>SMART goal</summary><p>A goal that is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.</p></details></section><p>A career plan is a living document that connects your current skills to your target role through a sequence of dated actions. It combines your self-assessment, your SMART goals, and the specific credentials, experiences, and contacts you need to acquire. Reviewed each quarter, it keeps you moving deliberately instead of drifting. The best plans are short, specific, and revised as the market and your interests evolve.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Name your target role and the sector it sits in.</li><li>List the credentials and experiences that role requires.</li><li>Map each gap to a concrete action with a deadline.</li><li>Identify people and networks who can open doors.</li><li>Schedule a quarterly review to update the plan.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Aiming for a CDC fellowship, a graduate plans two years: publish one paper, present at an APHA meeting, and complete an outbreak field placement.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A written plan turns intention into steady, visible progress that hiring managers and mentors can recognize and support.</p></div>",
 "Epidemiology Essentials": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Epidemiology Essentials</strong><br><span>The science of disease patterns</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-skills-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Epidemiology Essentials\"><figcaption>Epidemiology Essentials: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Concept</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Epidemiology Essentials.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Public-health use</strong><span>Apply Epidemiology Essentials to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Epidemiology Essentials</summary><p>Epidemiology Essentials is the focus of this Core Public Health Skills Employers Want lesson. It develops the learner's ability to apply core technical knowledge in real health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>population health</summary><p>The health outcomes of a group and their distribution within that group.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>health indicator</summary><p>A measurable value used to describe health status or programme performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based practice</summary><p>Decision-making guided by reliable research, data, and professional judgment.</p></details></section><p>Epidemiology is the study of how diseases and health conditions are distributed in populations and what causes those patterns. It underpins nearly every public health decision, from outbreak response to policy. Core concepts include measures of frequency like incidence and prevalence, measures of association like relative risk, and study designs such as cohort and case-control. Mastering this vocabulary lets you read the evidence and act on it.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Incidence</strong> – new cases over a period.</li><li><strong>Prevalence</strong> – existing cases at a point in time.</li><li><strong>Relative risk</strong> – compares risk between groups.</li><li><strong>Study design</strong> – cohort, case-control, cross-sectional.</li><li><strong>Confounding</strong> – a third factor distorting associations.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A CDC Epidemic Intelligence Service officer uses a case-control study to link a multistate Salmonella outbreak to a single contaminated produce supplier.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Epidemiologic fluency is the core competency employers screen for across CDC, state agencies, and industry roles.</p></div>",
 "Biostatistics and Data Literacy": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Biostatistics and Data Literacy</strong><br><span>Turning numbers into evidence</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-skills-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Biostatistics and Data Literacy\"><figcaption>Biostatistics and Data Literacy: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Concept</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Biostatistics and Data Literacy.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Public-health use</strong><span>Apply Biostatistics and Data Literacy to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Biostatistics and Data Literacy</summary><p>Biostatistics and Data Literacy is the focus of this Core Public Health Skills Employers Want lesson. It develops the learner's ability to apply core technical knowledge in real health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>population health</summary><p>The health outcomes of a group and their distribution within that group.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>health indicator</summary><p>A measurable value used to describe health status or programme performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based practice</summary><p>Decision-making guided by reliable research, data, and professional judgment.</p></details></section><p>Biostatistics gives public health its analytical backbone, providing the methods to summarize data, quantify uncertainty, and test hypotheses. Data literacy is the broader ability to find, clean, interpret, and communicate data responsibly. Together they let you tell whether an observed difference is real or due to chance, and whether a finding should change practice. Modern employers expect comfort with software such as R, SAS, or Stata.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Descriptive statistics</strong> – means, medians, and spread.</li><li><strong>P-values</strong> – evidence against a null hypothesis.</li><li><strong>Confidence intervals</strong> – a plausible range for an estimate.</li><li><strong>Regression</strong> – models relationships and adjusts for confounders.</li><li><strong>Data cleaning</strong> – ensuring quality before analysis.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A biostatistician on an NIH-funded study uses logistic regression in R to estimate how a new screening program changed early cancer detection rates.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Strong data skills are among the most rewarded and transferable assets across government, academia, and pharma.</p></div>",
 "Disease Surveillance": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Disease Surveillance</strong><br><span>The early warning system for health</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-skills-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Disease Surveillance\"><figcaption>Disease Surveillance: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Concept</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Disease Surveillance.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Public-health use</strong><span>Apply Disease Surveillance to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Disease Surveillance</summary><p>Disease Surveillance is the focus of this Core Public Health Skills Employers Want lesson. It develops the learner's ability to apply core technical knowledge in real health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>population health</summary><p>The health outcomes of a group and their distribution within that group.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>health indicator</summary><p>A measurable value used to describe health status or programme performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based practice</summary><p>Decision-making guided by reliable research, data, and professional judgment.</p></details></section><p>Disease surveillance is the ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, and interpretation of health data used to plan and evaluate public health action. It detects outbreaks early, tracks trends, and guides where to direct resources. Systems range from mandatory case reporting of notifiable diseases to syndromic surveillance that watches emergency department visits in near real time. Timely, accurate surveillance is the difference between a contained cluster and a widespread epidemic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Passive surveillance</strong> – routine provider case reports.</li><li><strong>Active surveillance</strong> – agencies seek cases directly.</li><li><strong>Syndromic surveillance</strong> – monitors symptoms in real time.</li><li><strong>Notifiable diseases</strong> – conditions legally reported to health departments.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A county epidemiologist in California spots a rise in measles reports through the state notifiable disease system and launches contact tracing within a day.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Surveillance roles at CDC, CSTE, and state health departments are steady, high-impact entry points for new graduates.</p></div>",
 "Health Promotion and Education": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Health Promotion and Education</strong><br><span>Helping people live healthier</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-skills-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Health Promotion and Education\"><figcaption>Health Promotion and Education: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Concept</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Health Promotion and Education.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Public-health use</strong><span>Apply Health Promotion and Education to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Health Promotion and Education</summary><p>Health Promotion and Education is the focus of this Core Public Health Skills Employers Want lesson. It develops the learner's ability to apply core technical knowledge in real health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>population health</summary><p>The health outcomes of a group and their distribution within that group.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>health indicator</summary><p>A measurable value used to describe health status or programme performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based practice</summary><p>Decision-making guided by reliable research, data, and professional judgment.</p></details></section><p>Health promotion is the process of enabling people and communities to increase control over and improve their health. Health education, one of its main tools, uses evidence-based communication and behavior-change theory to shift knowledge, attitudes, and behaviors. Practitioners design campaigns, run community programs, and evaluate whether those efforts actually change outcomes over time. Effective work here blends behavioral science with cultural insight, trusted messengers, and careful measurement of what truly moves the needle.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Behavior-change theory</strong> – models like the Health Belief Model.</li><li><strong>Health literacy</strong> – making information understandable.</li><li><strong>Community engagement</strong> – involving those affected.</li><li><strong>Program evaluation</strong> – measuring real impact.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A health educator at a Massachusetts county health department runs a school-based vaping prevention campaign and evaluates it with pre and post student surveys.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Certified Health Education Specialist roles are in demand across health departments, hospitals, and nonprofits nationwide.</p></div>",
 "Health Systems and Policy": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Health Systems and Policy</strong><br><span>How care gets organized and funded</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-skills-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Health Systems and Policy\"><figcaption>Health Systems and Policy: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Concept</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Health Systems and Policy.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Public-health use</strong><span>Apply Health Systems and Policy to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Health Systems and Policy</summary><p>Health Systems and Policy is the focus of this Core Public Health Skills Employers Want lesson. It develops the learner's ability to apply core technical knowledge in real health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>population health</summary><p>The health outcomes of a group and their distribution within that group.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>health indicator</summary><p>A measurable value used to describe health status or programme performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based practice</summary><p>Decision-making guided by reliable research, data, and professional judgment.</p></details></section><p>Health systems are the organizations, people, and resources that deliver health services, and health policy sets the rules and priorities that shape them. Understanding financing, coverage, and regulation helps public health professionals design interventions that fit real institutions. In the United States that means grasping Medicare, Medicaid, private insurance, and agencies like HHS and CMS; comparable systems include the UK NHS and Canada's provincial plans. Policy analysis translates evidence into law and budgets.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Financing</strong> – how care is paid for and covered.</li><li><strong>Access</strong> – who can obtain needed services.</li><li><strong>Regulation</strong> – rules governing quality and safety.</li><li><strong>Policy analysis</strong> – weighing options against evidence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A policy analyst at a US state health department models how expanding Medicaid coverage would affect uninsured rates and emergency department use.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Policy and systems expertise opens leadership tracks in government, think tanks, and health administration.</p></div>",
 "Environmental and Occupational Health": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Environmental and Occupational Health</strong><br><span>Protecting people where they live and work</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-skills-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Environmental and Occupational Health\"><figcaption>Environmental and Occupational Health: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Concept</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Environmental and Occupational Health.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Public-health use</strong><span>Apply Environmental and Occupational Health to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Environmental and Occupational Health</summary><p>Environmental and Occupational Health is the focus of this Core Public Health Skills Employers Want lesson. It develops the learner's ability to apply core technical knowledge in real health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>population health</summary><p>The health outcomes of a group and their distribution within that group.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>health indicator</summary><p>A measurable value used to describe health status or programme performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based practice</summary><p>Decision-making guided by reliable research, data, and professional judgment.</p></details></section><p>Environmental health addresses how air, water, food, and built surroundings affect wellbeing, while occupational health focuses on hazards in the workplace. Practitioners assess exposures, enforce standards, and prevent illness and injury before it happens. In the United States, agencies such as the EPA, OSHA, and NIOSH set and enforce the protective rules that this field puts into daily practice. The work blends toxicology, exposure science, risk assessment, and regulation to keep communities and workers safe.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Exposure assessment</strong> – measuring contact with hazards.</li><li><strong>Risk assessment</strong> – estimating harm from exposures.</li><li><strong>Regulation</strong> – EPA, OSHA, and NIOSH standards.</li><li><strong>Prevention</strong> – controls that reduce exposure at the source.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An environmental health specialist investigates elevated lead levels in a Michigan city's water supply and recommends corrosion controls to protect residents.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Environmental and occupational roles offer stable careers across federal agencies, state programs, and private industry.</p></div>",
 "Global Health and One Health": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Global Health and One Health</strong><br><span>Health without borders</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-skills-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Global Health and One Health\"><figcaption>Global Health and One Health: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Concept</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Global Health and One Health.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Public-health use</strong><span>Apply Global Health and One Health to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Global Health and One Health</summary><p>Global Health and One Health is the focus of this Core Public Health Skills Employers Want lesson. It develops the learner's ability to apply core technical knowledge in real health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>population health</summary><p>The health outcomes of a group and their distribution within that group.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>health indicator</summary><p>A measurable value used to describe health status or programme performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>evidence-based practice</summary><p>Decision-making guided by reliable research, data, and professional judgment.</p></details></section><p>Global health examines health issues that transcend national boundaries and require international cooperation, from pandemics to chronic disease. One Health is a complementary approach recognizing that human, animal, and environmental health are deeply connected, which matters greatly for emerging zoonotic diseases. Together they frame how developed countries and global agencies coordinate surveillance, research, and response. Careers here span agencies, universities, and multinational organizations.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Global agencies</strong> – WHO, UN, and the ECDC in Europe.</li><li><strong>Zoonoses</strong> – diseases passing between animals and people.</li><li><strong>Health equity</strong> – closing gaps within and across nations.</li><li><strong>Cross-sector collaboration</strong> – uniting human, animal, and environmental sectors.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A US CDC scientist working with the WHO applies a One Health approach to track avian influenza across poultry farms and human contacts.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Global and One Health expertise is increasingly sought at CDC, WHO, and international research institutions.</p></div>",
 "Writing a Public Health CV": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writing a Public Health CV</strong><br><span>Resume for most US jobs, CV for academia and global roles</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-documents-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Writing a Public Health CV\"><figcaption>Writing a Public Health CV: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Writing a Public Health CV.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Apply Writing a Public Health CV to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Professional result</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Writing a Public Health CV</summary><p>Writing a Public Health CV is the focus of this Your Professional Documents lesson. It develops the learner's ability to present qualifications clearly in strong professional documents.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>achievement statement</summary><p>A concise description of an action and its measurable result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional profile</summary><p>A short summary of relevant strengths, experience, and goals.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tailoring</summary><p>Adapting a document to the requirements of a specific opportunity.</p></details></section><p>In the United States, most public health employers expect a one-to-two page resume, not a long CV. A resume is a concise, tailored summary of relevant experience. A CV (curriculum vitae) is longer and lists publications, presentations, and grants; it is standard for academic and many WHO or UN postings. Know which document an employer wants before applying.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the posting and mirror its keywords, since many employers screen through applicant tracking systems.</li><li>Lead with a header, then a summary, education, and experience in reverse order.</li><li>Write bullets that quantify impact, such as screened 400 patients or cut clinic wait times by 20 percent.</li><li>Add skills like SAS, R, or biostatistics relevant to the role.</li><li>Keep a resume to two pages; expand to a full CV for academic jobs.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a CDC epidemiologist posting on USAJOBS, tailor a federal resume that echoes the announcement language and your outbreak analysis work.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A targeted resume or CV is your first screen; getting the format right for each employer decides whether you reach the interview.</p></div>",
 "Writing a Cover Letter": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writing a Cover Letter</strong><br><span>Connect your story to one specific role</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-documents-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Writing a Cover Letter\"><figcaption>Writing a Cover Letter: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Writing a Cover Letter.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Apply Writing a Cover Letter to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Professional result</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Writing a Cover Letter</summary><p>Writing a Cover Letter is the focus of this Your Professional Documents lesson. It develops the learner's ability to present qualifications clearly in strong professional documents.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>achievement statement</summary><p>A concise description of an action and its measurable result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional profile</summary><p>A short summary of relevant strengths, experience, and goals.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tailoring</summary><p>Adapting a document to the requirements of a specific opportunity.</p></details></section><p>A cover letter is a one-page narrative that tells an employer why you fit a particular public health role and why you want it. Unlike your resume, it lets you show voice and motivation. Strong letters are tailored to each posting, name the organization, and translate your experience into value for that team. Generic letters are easy to spot and quickly discarded by US hiring managers.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Address a named person when possible, such as the hiring manager or program director.</li><li>Open with the exact role and where you saw it, plus a hook about your fit.</li><li>In two body paragraphs, match your top achievements to the job requirements with concrete evidence.</li><li>Show you researched the employer by referencing a program, value, or recent initiative.</li><li>Close with a confident call to action and thank the reader.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a county epidemiologist role in California, cite your surveillance experience and connect it to the department's chronic disease priorities.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A tailored cover letter can lift a borderline application into the interview pile by showing genuine fit and effort.</p></div>",
 "Personal Statements and Motivation Letters": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Personal Statements and Motivation Letters</strong><br><span>Your purpose, told with evidence</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-documents-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Personal Statements and Motivation Letters\"><figcaption>Personal Statements and Motivation Letters: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Personal Statements and Motivation Letters.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Apply Personal Statements and Motivation Letters to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Professional result</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Personal Statements and Motivation Letters</summary><p>Personal Statements and Motivation Letters is the focus of this Your Professional Documents lesson. It develops the learner's ability to present qualifications clearly in strong professional documents.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>achievement statement</summary><p>A concise description of an action and its measurable result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional profile</summary><p>A short summary of relevant strengths, experience, and goals.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tailoring</summary><p>Adapting a document to the requirements of a specific opportunity.</p></details></section><p>Personal statements and motivation letters are used for graduate programs, fellowships, and many international roles. They explain who you are, what drives your commitment to public health, and where you want to go. Reviewers at US schools and funders read hundreds, so specificity and a clear arc matter more than lofty language. Show growth through real moments, not general claims about passion.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Open with a specific moment or problem that shaped your public health interest.</li><li>Trace a clear arc from that spark to your training and experience.</li><li>Name the specific program, fellowship, or organization and why it fits your goals.</li><li>Give concrete evidence of skills and impact rather than adjectives.</li><li>End with focused future goals and edit ruthlessly to the stated word limit.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Applying to the CDC's Epidemic Intelligence Service, describe a specific outbreak project and how the fellowship advances your applied epidemiology goals.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A focused, evidence-rich statement separates you from applicants with similar grades and helps selection committees remember you.</p></div>",
 "Building a Professional Portfolio": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building a Professional Portfolio</strong><br><span>Show the work, not just claims about it</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-documents-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Building a Professional Portfolio\"><figcaption>Building a Professional Portfolio: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Building a Professional Portfolio.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Apply Building a Professional Portfolio to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Professional result</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Building a Professional Portfolio</summary><p>Building a Professional Portfolio is the focus of this Your Professional Documents lesson. It develops the learner's ability to present qualifications clearly in strong professional documents.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>achievement statement</summary><p>A concise description of an action and its measurable result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional profile</summary><p>A short summary of relevant strengths, experience, and goals.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tailoring</summary><p>Adapting a document to the requirements of a specific opportunity.</p></details></section><p>A professional portfolio is a curated collection of your public health work that proves what your resume asserts. It can include reports, data visualizations, program evaluations, published briefs, and presentations. In the US job market, a portfolio is especially valuable for data, communications, and program roles where employers want to see deliverables. Host it simply and share the link on your resume and LinkedIn.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Gather your strongest deliverables: dashboards, policy briefs, evaluation reports, and talks.</li><li>Remove or anonymize any confidential or protected health information before sharing.</li><li>Organize samples by skill area with a short caption explaining your role and impact.</li><li>Host it on a clean site such as GitHub Pages, a personal website, or a PDF.</li><li>Keep it current and link it from your resume, LinkedIn, and applications.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A recent MPH graduate builds a GitHub Pages site featuring an R Shiny dashboard and a county immunization brief to support US data analyst applications.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Tangible work samples reduce employer risk and give you concrete talking points that set you apart in interviews.</p></div>",
 "Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile</strong><br><span>Be found by US recruiters</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-documents-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile\"><figcaption>Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Apply Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Professional result</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile</summary><p>Creating a Strong LinkedIn Profile is the focus of this Your Professional Documents lesson. It develops the learner's ability to present qualifications clearly in strong professional documents.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>achievement statement</summary><p>A concise description of an action and its measurable result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional profile</summary><p>A short summary of relevant strengths, experience, and goals.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tailoring</summary><p>Adapting a document to the requirements of a specific opportunity.</p></details></section><p>LinkedIn is the default professional network for US and other developed-country employers, and recruiters actively search it to fill public health roles. A strong profile is complete, keyword-rich, and consistent with your resume. It works around the clock, surfacing you for jobs you never applied to. Treat it as a living document rather than a one-time setup.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Use a professional headshot and a headline naming your field, such as Epidemiologist and Data Analyst.</li><li>Write an About section in first person that summarizes your focus and key wins.</li><li>Fill Experience with quantified bullets and add skills recruiters search, like SAS and program evaluation.</li><li>Set your location to your target US market and turn on Open to Work quietly.</li><li>Request recommendations and post or comment on public health topics to stay visible.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate targeting US health departments adds keywords like surveillance and biostatistics, then connects with CDC and APHA members to grow reach.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Recruiters at CDC, NIH, and consulting firms source candidates on LinkedIn daily, so an optimized profile brings opportunities to you.</p></div>",
 "References and Recommendation Letters": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 References and Recommendation Letters</strong><br><span>People who vouch for your work</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-documents-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for References and Recommendation Letters\"><figcaption>References and Recommendation Letters: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Purpose</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of References and Recommendation Letters.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Apply References and Recommendation Letters to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Professional result</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>References and Recommendation Letters</summary><p>References and Recommendation Letters is the focus of this Your Professional Documents lesson. It develops the learner's ability to present qualifications clearly in strong professional documents.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>achievement statement</summary><p>A concise description of an action and its measurable result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional profile</summary><p>A short summary of relevant strengths, experience, and goals.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>tailoring</summary><p>Adapting a document to the requirements of a specific opportunity.</p></details></section><p>References and recommendation letters let others confirm your skills and character. US employers typically request three professional references late in hiring, while graduate programs and fellowships often require written letters. The best referees know your work well and can speak to specific results. Managing this process politely and early protects your reputation and your relationships.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Choose referees who supervised your public health work and can cite concrete achievements.</li><li>Ask permission early and confirm they can speak positively about you.</li><li>Share your resume, the job or program details, and points you would like emphasized.</li><li>Give letter writers at least three to four weeks and clear submission instructions.</li><li>Send a thank-you and update referees on your outcome.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a state health department analyst role, a graduate lists a practicum preceptor and a professor, briefing each on the posting before the reference call.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Strong, well-prepared references confirm your fit at the final stage and can tip a close hiring decision in your favor.</p></div>",
 "Where to Find Public Health Jobs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Where to Find Public Health Jobs</strong><br><span>Know the right doors to knock on</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-jobsearch-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Where to Find Public Health Jobs\"><figcaption>Where to Find Public Health Jobs: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Where to Find Public Health Jobs.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Research</strong><span>Apply Where to Find Public Health Jobs to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Where to Find Public Health Jobs</summary><p>Where to Find Public Health Jobs is the focus of this Finding the Right Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to identify suitable opportunities and build relationships with employers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>job criteria</summary><p>The qualifications, skills, and experience required for a role.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>networking</summary><p>Building mutually useful professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>application tracking</summary><p>Recording vacancies, deadlines, submissions, and follow-up actions.</p></details></section><p>Public health jobs in the US and other developed countries are spread across government portals, professional associations, and general job boards. Each channel favors different roles, so searching in one place limits your reach. The most effective seekers combine several sources, set alerts, and check them on a regular schedule. Knowing where each sector posts saves weeks of wasted effort.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>For US federal roles at CDC, NIH, or FDA, search and apply on USAJOBS.</li><li>For state, county, and local health department jobs, check government HR sites and NACCHO listings.</li><li>Use association boards such as APHA Career Mart and Idealist for nonprofit and program roles.</li><li>Search LinkedIn and Indeed for private, consulting, and academic positions.</li><li>For UK, Canada, and global roles, use NHS Jobs, Canada PHAC, and WHO or UN careers pages.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate seeking an epidemiology job monitors USAJOBS for CDC postings, APHA Career Mart for nonprofits, and Indeed for hospital roles weekly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Matching the sector to the right platform dramatically increases how many suitable openings you actually see.</p></div>",
 "Working with NGOs and INGOs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Working with NGOs and INGOs</strong><br><span>Mission-driven public health careers</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-jobsearch-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Working with NGOs and INGOs\"><figcaption>Working with NGOs and INGOs: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Working with NGOs and INGOs.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Research</strong><span>Apply Working with NGOs and INGOs to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Working with NGOs and INGOs</summary><p>Working with NGOs and INGOs is the focus of this Finding the Right Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to identify suitable opportunities and build relationships with employers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>job criteria</summary><p>The qualifications, skills, and experience required for a role.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>networking</summary><p>Building mutually useful professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>application tracking</summary><p>Recording vacancies, deadlines, submissions, and follow-up actions.</p></details></section><p>Nongovernmental and international nongovernmental organizations deliver much of the world's public health programming and hire heavily in the US and Europe. Groups like the American Red Cross, PATH, FHI 360, and Doctors Without Borders offer roles in program management, monitoring and evaluation, and field work. These employers value adaptability, grant awareness, and measurable results. Understanding how they operate helps you target and win these positions.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify NGOs whose mission matches your focus, such as maternal health or infectious disease.</li><li>Search their careers pages plus Idealist and Devex for openings.</li><li>Tailor your resume to their program cycle language: needs assessment, implementation, and evaluation.</li><li>Highlight funding literacy, since many roles support USAID, Gates Foundation, or CDC grants.</li><li>Network with staff on LinkedIn and attend APHA or Global Health Council events.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate applies to a PATH program officer role in Seattle, framing practicum work as monitoring and evaluation aligned to donor reporting.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>NGOs and INGOs are major public health employers, and speaking their program and funding language makes you a credible candidate.</p></div>",
 "Ministry of Health and Government Roles": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Ministry of Health and Government Roles</strong><br><span>Federal, state, and local public health careers</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-jobsearch-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Ministry of Health and Government Roles\"><figcaption>Ministry of Health and Government Roles: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Ministry of Health and Government Roles.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Research</strong><span>Apply Ministry of Health and Government Roles to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Ministry of Health and Government Roles</summary><p>Ministry of Health and Government Roles is the focus of this Finding the Right Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to identify suitable opportunities and build relationships with employers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>job criteria</summary><p>The qualifications, skills, and experience required for a role.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>networking</summary><p>Building mutually useful professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>application tracking</summary><p>Recording vacancies, deadlines, submissions, and follow-up actions.</p></details></section><p>Government agencies employ the largest share of public health workers in developed countries. In the US this spans federal bodies like the CDC, HHS, and FDA, state health departments, and county or city health departments. Equivalents abroad include the UK's NHS and UKHSA and Canada's PHAC. These roles offer stability, benefits, and mission impact, but hiring follows structured, keyword-driven processes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Decide your level: federal for national programs, state for policy and surveillance, local for direct services.</li><li>Apply to US federal jobs through USAJOBS with a detailed federal-style resume.</li><li>Check state and county HR portals and NACCHO for local health department openings.</li><li>Mirror the announcement's required qualifications and keywords to pass automated screening.</li><li>For abroad, use NHS Jobs, UKHSA, and Canada PHAC portals with their formats.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate applies for a county health department disease intervention specialist role, echoing the posting's contact tracing and surveillance language.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Government agencies hire at scale and reward applicants who navigate their formal, keyword-heavy processes correctly.</p></div>",
 "WHO, UN and International Agencies": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 WHO, UN and International Agencies</strong><br><span>Global public health at the multilateral level</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-jobsearch-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for WHO, UN and International Agencies\"><figcaption>WHO, UN and International Agencies: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of WHO, UN and International Agencies.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Research</strong><span>Apply WHO, UN and International Agencies to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>WHO, UN and International Agencies</summary><p>WHO, UN and International Agencies is the focus of this Finding the Right Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to identify suitable opportunities and build relationships with employers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>job criteria</summary><p>The qualifications, skills, and experience required for a role.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>networking</summary><p>Building mutually useful professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>application tracking</summary><p>Recording vacancies, deadlines, submissions, and follow-up actions.</p></details></section><p>The World Health Organization, UNICEF, and other UN agencies shape global health policy and run large programs. They recruit from the US and other developed countries for technical, coordination, and research roles. Competition is intense, applications use structured online systems, and roles often require advanced degrees, languages, and international experience. Understanding their processes early helps you build the right profile.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Explore agency careers portals such as WHO Careers, UN Careers, and UNICEF.</li><li>Learn the UN competency framework and grade system, from entry-level P grades upward.</li><li>Build a full CV covering publications, languages, and international work.</li><li>Consider entry paths like the UN Volunteers program, JPO schemes, and internships.</li><li>Complete the detailed online application and personal history form carefully and on time.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A US graduate applies for a WHO technical officer position, aligning their CV to the vacancy's required competencies and language requirements.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>UN roles are prestigious and highly competitive, so early awareness of their systems and requirements gives you a realistic path in.</p></div>",
 "Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering</strong><br><span>Early experience that opens doors</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-jobsearch-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering\"><figcaption>Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Research</strong><span>Apply Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering</summary><p>Fellowships, Internships and Volunteering is the focus of this Finding the Right Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to identify suitable opportunities and build relationships with employers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>job criteria</summary><p>The qualifications, skills, and experience required for a role.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>networking</summary><p>Building mutually useful professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>application tracking</summary><p>Recording vacancies, deadlines, submissions, and follow-up actions.</p></details></section><p>Fellowships, internships, and volunteering build the applied experience that entry-level public health jobs demand. In the US these include the CDC's Epidemic Intelligence Service, ORISE fellowships, and Presidential Management Fellows. Many convert into full-time roles or unlock networks and references. Treating them as strategic career moves rather than filler time accelerates your trajectory.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify programs matching your stage, such as ORISE and EIS for graduates.</li><li>Track deadlines early, since fellowships like EIS run annual cycles.</li><li>Prepare strong statements, references, and a tailored resume for each application.</li><li>During the placement, deliver measurable work and collect a portfolio and referees.</li><li>Use the network you build to learn about and apply for permanent roles.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A new MPH graduate takes an ORISE fellowship at the CDC, then leverages the projects and references to land a permanent epidemiologist role.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>These experiences supply the hands-on evidence and connections that turn a strong degree into a competitive job candidacy.</p></div>",
 "Networking for Public Health Professionals": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Networking for Public Health Professionals</strong><br><span>Relationships that surface hidden jobs</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-jobsearch-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Networking for Public Health Professionals\"><figcaption>Networking for Public Health Professionals: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Networking for Public Health Professionals.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Research</strong><span>Apply Networking for Public Health Professionals to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Networking for Public Health Professionals</summary><p>Networking for Public Health Professionals is the focus of this Finding the Right Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to identify suitable opportunities and build relationships with employers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>job criteria</summary><p>The qualifications, skills, and experience required for a role.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>networking</summary><p>Building mutually useful professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>application tracking</summary><p>Recording vacancies, deadlines, submissions, and follow-up actions.</p></details></section><p>Many public health roles are filled through referrals before they are widely advertised. Networking means building genuine professional relationships, not asking strangers for jobs. In the US, conferences, alumni networks, and LinkedIn are the main channels. Consistent, generous outreach compounds over time into mentors, referrals, and insider knowledge about openings.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Join professional bodies like APHA and attend their annual meeting and section events.</li><li>Reach out to alumni and practitioners for short informational interviews.</li><li>Engage on LinkedIn by commenting on and sharing public health work thoughtfully.</li><li>Follow up promptly, offer help in return, and stay in touch beyond immediate needs.</li><li>Keep a simple contact tracker so relationships do not go cold.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">At the APHA Annual Meeting, a graduate requests a fifteen-minute informational chat with a state epidemiologist and later gets alerted to an opening.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A strong network gives you early access to unadvertised roles and trusted referrals that outperform cold applications.</p></div>",
 "Using Job Boards and Alerts": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Using Job Boards and Alerts</strong><br><span>Let the right jobs come to you</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-jobsearch-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Using Job Boards and Alerts\"><figcaption>Using Job Boards and Alerts: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Target</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Using Job Boards and Alerts.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Research</strong><span>Apply Using Job Boards and Alerts to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Connect</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Using Job Boards and Alerts</summary><p>Using Job Boards and Alerts is the focus of this Finding the Right Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to identify suitable opportunities and build relationships with employers.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>job criteria</summary><p>The qualifications, skills, and experience required for a role.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>networking</summary><p>Building mutually useful professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>application tracking</summary><p>Recording vacancies, deadlines, submissions, and follow-up actions.</p></details></section><p>Job boards aggregate openings, but the seekers who succeed automate their search with alerts and refined filters. Instead of browsing manually every day, you set precise criteria and let notifications surface matches. This saves time and ensures you apply early, which matters because many public health postings close quickly or review on a rolling basis.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Pick your core boards: USAJOBS, LinkedIn, Indeed, APHA Career Mart, and Idealist.</li><li>Use specific search terms and filters such as epidemiologist, remote, and entry level.</li><li>Save the searches and enable email or app alerts on each platform.</li><li>Review alerts daily and apply quickly, since strong roles fill fast.</li><li>Refine keywords over time based on which alerts return relevant results.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A graduate sets a USAJOBS alert for CDC health scientist postings and a LinkedIn alert for remote biostatistician roles, applying within a day of each.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Automated alerts let you apply early and consistently without daily manual searching, giving you an edge on time-sensitive openings.</p></div>",
 "Preparing for a Public Health Interview": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Preparing for a Public Health Interview</strong><br><span>Walk in ready, not rattled</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-interview-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Preparing for a Public Health Interview\"><figcaption>Preparing for a Public Health Interview: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prepare</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Preparing for a Public Health Interview.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Respond</strong><span>Apply Preparing for a Public Health Interview to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow up</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Preparing for a Public Health Interview</summary><p>Preparing for a Public Health Interview is the focus of this Winning the Interview lesson. It develops the learner's ability to answer public-health interview questions with evidence and confidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>competency</summary><p>A combination of knowledge, skill, and behavior needed for effective performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>STAR method</summary><p>A response structure covering situation, task, action, and result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>panel interview</summary><p>An interview conducted by multiple interviewers.</p></details></section><p>Strong interview preparation turns a stressful conversation into a focused exchange where you demonstrate fit. For public health roles at agencies like the CDC, NIH, or a state health department, employers want evidence that you understand their mission and can apply skills such as epidemiology, program evaluation, or health communication. Preparation lowers anxiety and sharpens your answers so you sound deliberate rather than improvised.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Research the organization, its funding streams, and recent public health priorities or reports.</li><li>Reread the job description and map each requirement to a concrete accomplishment from your resume.</li><li>Prepare five to seven stories covering leadership, teamwork, data, and problem solving.</li><li>Practice out loud with a mock interviewer and time your responses.</li><li>Prepare thoughtful questions and confirm logistics, dress, and technology in advance.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Before a county health department epidemiologist interview, a candidate reviews the county community health assessment, then drafts a story about cleaning a surveillance dataset in Excel that cut reporting errors by 30 percent.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Prepared candidates project confidence and competence, which directly improves offer rates in competitive US public health hiring.</p></div>",
 "Common Interview Questions and Answers": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Common Interview Questions and Answers</strong><br><span>Anticipate, structure, deliver</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-interview-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Common Interview Questions and Answers\"><figcaption>Common Interview Questions and Answers: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prepare</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Common Interview Questions and Answers.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Respond</strong><span>Apply Common Interview Questions and Answers to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow up</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Common Interview Questions and Answers</summary><p>Common Interview Questions and Answers is the focus of this Winning the Interview lesson. It develops the learner's ability to answer public-health interview questions with evidence and confidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>competency</summary><p>A combination of knowledge, skill, and behavior needed for effective performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>STAR method</summary><p>A response structure covering situation, task, action, and result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>panel interview</summary><p>An interview conducted by multiple interviewers.</p></details></section><p>Most public health interviews recycle a predictable set of questions, so preparing crisp responses gives you a real advantage. Interviewers at hospitals, universities, and federal agencies use these prompts to gauge motivation, self-awareness, and technical grounding. Knowing the patterns lets you answer with structure instead of scrambling for words under pressure.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>For Tell me about yourself, give a 90-second arc: background, key skills, and why this role.</li><li>For strengths, name a skill and back it with a measurable result.</li><li>For weaknesses, choose a real gap plus the steps you are taking to close it.</li><li>For Why this organization, tie their mission to your values and prior work.</li><li>Close every answer with impact, not just activity.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Asked why she wants an NIH research coordinator role, a candidate connects her thesis on diabetes disparities to the institute's chronic disease portfolio, then cites her REDCap data management experience as directly transferable.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Polished answers to common questions free your energy for the harder, role-specific questions that decide the hire.</p></div>",
 "Behavioral (STAR) Interviews": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Behavioral (STAR) Interviews</strong><br><span>Past behavior predicts future performance</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-interview-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Behavioral (STAR) Interviews\"><figcaption>Behavioral (STAR) Interviews: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prepare</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Behavioral (STAR) Interviews.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Respond</strong><span>Apply Behavioral (STAR) Interviews to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow up</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Behavioral (STAR) Interviews</summary><p>Behavioral (STAR) Interviews is the focus of this Winning the Interview lesson. It develops the learner's ability to answer public-health interview questions with evidence and confidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>competency</summary><p>A combination of knowledge, skill, and behavior needed for effective performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>STAR method</summary><p>A response structure covering situation, task, action, and result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>panel interview</summary><p>An interview conducted by multiple interviewers.</p></details></section><p>Behavioral interviews ask you to describe real situations because employers believe how you acted before predicts how you will act again. The STAR method keeps your answer organized and evidence-based, which is exactly what hiring panels at the CDC or a large US hospital system reward. Rambling stories lose interviewers; structured ones land.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Situation: set the context in one or two sentences.</li><li>Task: state the specific challenge or goal you owned.</li><li>Action: describe what you personally did, using I rather than we.</li><li>Result: quantify the outcome and note what you learned.</li><li>Rehearse six to eight STAR stories mapped to common competencies.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Situation: a state immunization dashboard showed a data lag. Task: I had to restore weekly reporting. Action: I rebuilt the Excel pipeline and trained two clinics on timely entry. Result: reporting lag dropped from ten days to two, improving outbreak response.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>STAR answers give interviewers concrete proof of your skills, making you far more memorable than candidates who speak in generalities.</p></div>",
 "Technical and Case Interviews": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Technical and Case Interviews</strong><br><span>Show your reasoning, not just the answer</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-interview-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Technical and Case Interviews\"><figcaption>Technical and Case Interviews: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prepare</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Technical and Case Interviews.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Respond</strong><span>Apply Technical and Case Interviews to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow up</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Technical and Case Interviews</summary><p>Technical and Case Interviews is the focus of this Winning the Interview lesson. It develops the learner's ability to answer public-health interview questions with evidence and confidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>competency</summary><p>A combination of knowledge, skill, and behavior needed for effective performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>STAR method</summary><p>A response structure covering situation, task, action, and result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>panel interview</summary><p>An interview conducted by multiple interviewers.</p></details></section><p>Technical and case interviews test whether you can apply public health methods to real problems on the spot. You might be asked to interpret an epidemiological curve, design a program evaluation, or reason through a hypothetical outbreak. Interviewers care as much about your thought process as your final answer, so narrate your logic clearly.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Restate the problem and clarify assumptions before diving in.</li><li>Structure your approach out loud, breaking it into logical stages.</li><li>Reference relevant methods, study designs, or tools like SPSS or R.</li><li>State limitations and how you would validate your conclusion.</li><li>Summarize a clear recommendation at the end.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Given rising foodborne illness reports in a metro area, a candidate outlines a case-control study, defines cases, proposes calculating odds ratios, and notes she would verify results against lab surveillance before advising the county on a source.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Employers hire analysts who reason transparently under uncertainty, so a clear methodical process signals you can be trusted with real data decisions.</p></div>",
 "Virtual and Panel Interviews": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Virtual and Panel Interviews</strong><br><span>Command the room, even on a screen</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-interview-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Virtual and Panel Interviews\"><figcaption>Virtual and Panel Interviews: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prepare</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Virtual and Panel Interviews.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Respond</strong><span>Apply Virtual and Panel Interviews to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow up</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Virtual and Panel Interviews</summary><p>Virtual and Panel Interviews is the focus of this Winning the Interview lesson. It develops the learner's ability to answer public-health interview questions with evidence and confidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>competency</summary><p>A combination of knowledge, skill, and behavior needed for effective performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>STAR method</summary><p>A response structure covering situation, task, action, and result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>panel interview</summary><p>An interview conducted by multiple interviewers.</p></details></section><p>Virtual and panel formats are now standard for US public health hiring, especially at universities and federal agencies with distributed teams. These settings add technical and social complexity: you must manage video presence while engaging several interviewers at once. Handling them smoothly signals professionalism and adaptability.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Test your camera, microphone, lighting, and internet the day before.</li><li>Look into the camera to simulate eye contact, and keep a neutral background.</li><li>Address the panelist who asked, then briefly include the others.</li><li>Note each interviewer's name and tailor a closing question to their role.</li><li>Send individualized thank-you notes to every panel member afterward.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">During a Zoom panel for a university global health program officer, a candidate answers the data manager's technical question, then turns to the director to connect her answer to the program's strategic goals, showing she can read a room virtually.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Comfort on camera and with panels reassures employers you can collaborate across the remote and cross-functional teams common in modern public health.</p></div>",
 "Salary Negotiation": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Salary Negotiation</strong><br><span>Know your worth and ask well</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-interview-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Salary Negotiation\"><figcaption>Salary Negotiation: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prepare</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Salary Negotiation.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Respond</strong><span>Apply Salary Negotiation to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow up</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Salary Negotiation</summary><p>Salary Negotiation is the focus of this Winning the Interview lesson. It develops the learner's ability to answer public-health interview questions with evidence and confidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>competency</summary><p>A combination of knowledge, skill, and behavior needed for effective performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>STAR method</summary><p>A response structure covering situation, task, action, and result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>panel interview</summary><p>An interview conducted by multiple interviewers.</p></details></section><p>Negotiating salary is expected in the US job market, yet many public health graduates accept the first offer out of discomfort. A respectful, well-researched negotiation can raise your starting pay and future raises, since increases often compound off your base. Employers rarely rescind offers over a professional counter.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Research market ranges using BLS data, Glassdoor, and public salary schedules.</li><li>Let the employer name a number first when possible.</li><li>Anchor with a specific figure backed by your skills and market data.</li><li>Negotiate the full package: benefits, remote days, professional development.</li><li>Get the final agreement in writing before resigning elsewhere.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Offered 62,000 USD for a state epidemiologist role, a graduate cites regional ranges of 65,000 to 72,000 USD and her SAS analysis experience, then counters at 70,000. They settle at 68,000 USD plus a conference budget.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A single successful negotiation can add tens of thousands of dollars over a career, making this one of the highest-value skills you can practice.</p></div>",
 "Following Up After an Interview": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Following Up After an Interview</strong><br><span>The closing move that keeps you top of mind</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-interview-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Following Up After an Interview\"><figcaption>Following Up After an Interview: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Prepare</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Following Up After an Interview.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Respond</strong><span>Apply Following Up After an Interview to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Follow up</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Following Up After an Interview</summary><p>Following Up After an Interview is the focus of this Winning the Interview lesson. It develops the learner's ability to answer public-health interview questions with evidence and confidence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>competency</summary><p>A combination of knowledge, skill, and behavior needed for effective performance.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>STAR method</summary><p>A response structure covering situation, task, action, and result.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>panel interview</summary><p>An interview conducted by multiple interviewers.</p></details></section><p>A thoughtful follow-up reinforces your interest and gives you one more chance to make a strong impression. Many US hiring managers explicitly factor in whether candidates send timely, personalized notes. Done well, follow-up demonstrates professionalism without appearing pushy or desperate.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Send a thank-you email within 24 hours of the interview.</li><li>Reference a specific moment from the conversation to show you listened.</li><li>Restate your fit and briefly address any concern you sensed.</li><li>If you promised materials, attach or link them in the note.</li><li>Wait for the stated timeline before a single polite status check.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">After a CDC fellowship interview, a candidate emails the panel chair, thanks him, references their discussion of health equity metrics, and attaches a short sample Tableau dashboard she had mentioned building for a class project.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>In close hiring decisions, a strong follow-up can tip the balance and keep your name at the front of the panel's mind.</p></div>",
 "Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E)</strong><br><span>Proving whether programs actually work</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E)\"><figcaption>Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E): understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E).</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E) to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E)</summary><p>Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E) is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Monitoring and evaluation is the discipline of tracking program activities and measuring their results against goals. Monitoring is continuous and asks whether work is proceeding as planned, while evaluation is periodic and asks whether the program achieved its intended outcomes. US funders such as the CDC and NIH require rigorous M&E to justify continued funding and to guide improvements.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Logic model</strong> – maps inputs, activities, outputs, outcomes, and impact.</li><li><strong>Indicators</strong> – specific, measurable signals of progress, with baselines and targets.</li><li><strong>Monitoring</strong> – routine tracking of implementation and reach.</li><li><strong>Evaluation</strong> – process and outcome assessments of effectiveness.</li><li><strong>Data use</strong> – feeding findings back into decisions, not just reports.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a CDC-funded hypertension program, an M&E officer sets an indicator of patients with controlled blood pressure, tracks it quarterly in a dashboard, and evaluates whether the intervention beat the baseline after 18 months.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>M&E skills are in constant demand across US health agencies and nonprofits, since every funded program must demonstrate measurable results.</p></div>",
 "Project and Program Management": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Project and Program Management</strong><br><span>Turning plans into delivered results</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Project and Program Management\"><figcaption>Project and Program Management: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Project and Program Management.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Project and Program Management to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Project and Program Management</summary><p>Project and Program Management is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Project and program management is the practice of organizing people, budgets, and timelines to achieve defined public health objectives. A project is a time-bound effort with a clear deliverable, while a program is an ongoing collection of related projects sharing a strategic goal. Strong management keeps initiatives on scope, on schedule, and on budget.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Scope</strong> – the defined boundaries of what work is and is not included.</li><li><strong>Work plan</strong> – tasks, milestones, and dependencies on a timeline.</li><li><strong>Budget</strong> – forecasting and tracking spending against funding.</li><li><strong>Stakeholders</strong> – partners and sponsors whose buy-in you manage.</li><li><strong>Risk management</strong> – anticipating obstacles and planning responses.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A program manager at a state health department uses a Gantt chart in Microsoft Project to coordinate a vaccination outreach across five counties, tracking staffing, a 400,000 USD budget, and CDC reporting deadlines.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Employers value graduates who can move initiatives from proposal to completion, making project management a fast track to leadership roles.</p></div>",
 "Grant and Proposal Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Grant and Proposal Writing</strong><br><span>Winning the funding that powers programs</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Grant and Proposal Writing\"><figcaption>Grant and Proposal Writing: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Grant and Proposal Writing.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Grant and Proposal Writing to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Grant and Proposal Writing</summary><p>Grant and Proposal Writing is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Grant and proposal writing is how public health work gets funded in the US, from small foundation awards to multimillion-dollar NIH R01 grants. Reviewers score proposals against strict criteria, so a compelling narrative must pair scientific rigor with a clear, feasible plan. Learning this craft makes you invaluable to any funding-dependent organization.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Read the funding announcement and align every section to its review criteria.</li><li>Write a specific aims or problem statement that names the gap and your solution.</li><li>Justify significance with data, then detail a methodical, feasible approach.</li><li>Build a realistic budget and budget justification tied to activities.</li><li>Add evaluation, timeline, and a strong organizational capacity statement.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Applying for CDC funding to reduce childhood asthma ER visits, a writer states measurable aims, cites county surveillance data, proposes home-based interventions, and includes a logic model and a 750,000 USD three-year budget justification.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Organizations prize staff who can secure funding, so proposal writing skills often lead directly to promotions and job security.</p></div>",
 "Report Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Report Writing</strong><br><span>Communicating findings that drive action</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Report Writing\"><figcaption>Report Writing: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Report Writing.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Report Writing to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Report Writing</summary><p>Report Writing is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Report writing translates data and program activity into clear documents that inform decisions. Whether you are producing a quarterly progress report for a funder or a program evaluation for leadership, clarity and structure determine whether your findings are read and acted upon. In US public health, reports are a primary way your work becomes visible to decision makers.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify your audience and the decisions the report should support.</li><li>Open with an executive summary stating key findings and recommendations.</li><li>Present methods and results plainly, using tables and charts to aid clarity.</li><li>Interpret the data honestly, including limitations.</li><li>Close with specific, actionable recommendations and edit ruthlessly.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An analyst writing a quarterly report for a CDC-funded opioid program leads with an executive summary showing a 15 percent rise in naloxone distribution, supports it with a clear chart, and recommends expanding two high-uptake sites.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Clear reports amplify your impact and reputation, since leaders judge your work largely through the documents you produce.</p></div>",
 "Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK)": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK)</strong><br><span>Clean data starts at the source</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK)\"><figcaption>Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK): understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK).</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK) to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK)</summary><p>Data Collection Tools (KoBoToolbox & ODK) is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Digital data collection tools like KoBoToolbox and Open Data Kit let teams gather survey and field data on smartphones and tablets, even offline. They replace error-prone paper forms with structured, validated electronic forms that sync to a central server. In US settings, they complement platforms such as REDCap and Qualtrics for field surveys and rapid assessments.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Form design</strong> – build questions with skip logic and validation rules.</li><li><strong>Offline capture</strong> – collect data without connectivity, then sync later.</li><li><strong>Data validation</strong> – constraints reduce missing or impossible values at entry.</li><li><strong>Export</strong> – download to Excel, SPSS, or R for analysis.</li><li><strong>Data security</strong> – manage permissions and protect sensitive records.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A university research team uses KoBoToolbox on tablets to run a housing and health survey across several US neighborhoods, applying validation rules so age and income fields stay clean before exporting to SPSS.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Employers value staff who can deploy mobile data tools, because clean, timely field data underpins every credible analysis and report.</p></div>",
 "Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS</strong><br><span>From raw numbers to real insight</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS\"><figcaption>Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS</summary><p>Data Analysis with Excel and SPSS is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Excel and SPSS are workhorse tools for public health analysis in US agencies, hospitals, and universities. Excel handles data cleaning, pivot tables, and quick charts, while SPSS excels at statistical testing on larger datasets. Together they let you move from a messy spreadsheet to defensible findings that inform programs and publications.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Data cleaning</strong> – remove duplicates, fix types, and handle missing values first.</li><li><strong>Descriptive statistics</strong> – means, frequencies, and cross-tabs to summarize data.</li><li><strong>Pivot tables</strong> – Excel's fast tool for grouping and comparing.</li><li><strong>Inferential tests</strong> – chi-square, t-tests, and regression in SPSS.</li><li><strong>Documentation</strong> – keep a codebook and reproducible steps.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An epidemiologist at a county health department cleans a diabetes registry in Excel, builds pivot tables by age group, then runs a chi-square test in SPSS to check whether screening rates differ significantly by insurance status.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Practical Excel and SPSS skills appear in nearly every US public health job description, making them essential for employability and daily work.</p></div>",
 "Community Engagement and Mobilization": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Community Engagement and Mobilization</strong><br><span>Programs succeed when communities own them</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Community Engagement and Mobilization\"><figcaption>Community Engagement and Mobilization: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Community Engagement and Mobilization.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Community Engagement and Mobilization to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Community Engagement and Mobilization</summary><p>Community Engagement and Mobilization is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Community engagement is the practice of partnering with the people a program serves so interventions reflect their needs and earn their trust. Mobilization goes further, activating residents, coalitions, and local organizations to take collective action. In US public health, engaging communities is central to health equity work and is required by many federal and foundation funders.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Stakeholder mapping</strong> – identify who holds influence and who is affected.</li><li><strong>Trust building</strong> – engage early, listen, and follow through on commitments.</li><li><strong>Coalitions</strong> – bring partners together around shared goals.</li><li><strong>Cultural humility</strong> – adapt approaches to community values and language.</li><li><strong>Feedback loops</strong> – share results back and adjust based on input.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A city health department launching a diabetes prevention program convenes churches, clinics, and a resident advisory board, then co-designs outreach so messaging and class locations reflect what neighborhood members said they needed.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Employers seek professionals who can build authentic community partnerships, since sustained health improvements depend on genuine local ownership.</p></div>",
 "Building Data Dashboards": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building Data Dashboards</strong><br><span>Turning data into decisions at a glance</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-onjob-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Building Data Dashboards\"><figcaption>Building Data Dashboards: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Define</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Building Data Dashboards.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Apply</strong><span>Apply Building Data Dashboards to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Measure</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Building Data Dashboards</summary><p>Building Data Dashboards is the focus of this Skills You Use on the Job lesson. It develops the learner's ability to deliver, monitor, and improve public-health programmes.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>deliverable</summary><p>A completed product or result required by a project.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>indicator</summary><p>A measurable sign of progress, performance, or change.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>stakeholder</summary><p>A person or organization affected by or able to influence a programme.</p></details></section><p>Dashboards visualize key metrics so decision makers can monitor programs without digging through spreadsheets. Tools like Tableau and Power BI connect to live data and present trends, comparisons, and geographic patterns in interactive views. In US health departments and hospitals, dashboards have become the standard way to track surveillance, performance, and equity metrics in real time.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Audience focus</strong> – design for the decisions your viewers need to make.</li><li><strong>Key metrics</strong> – show a few high-value indicators, not everything.</li><li><strong>Clear visuals</strong> – choose chart types that match the data and avoid clutter.</li><li><strong>Interactivity</strong> – filters and drill-downs let users explore.</li><li><strong>Data refresh</strong> – automate updates and document sources.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An analyst builds a Tableau dashboard for a county health department showing weekly flu cases by ZIP code, vaccination coverage, and hospital capacity, with filters so managers can spot hotspots and shift resources quickly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Dashboard skills make your analysis visible and actionable, and Tableau or Power BI experience is increasingly listed as a preferred qualification.</p></div>",
 "Scientific and Technical Writing": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Scientific and Technical Writing</strong><br><span>Clear science, cleanly communicated</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-comm-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Scientific and Technical Writing\"><figcaption>Scientific and Technical Writing: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Audience</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Scientific and Technical Writing.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Message</strong><span>Apply Scientific and Technical Writing to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Action</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Scientific and Technical Writing</summary><p>Scientific and Technical Writing is the focus of this Communicating Like a Professional lesson. It develops the learner's ability to communicate public-health evidence clearly to technical and public audiences.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>key message</summary><p>A short, important point an audience should understand and remember.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>data visualization</summary><p>A chart, graph, or display used to communicate information.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>plain language</summary><p>Clear wording understandable to the intended audience.</p></details></section><p>Technical writing turns complex public health data into documents readers can act on. Whether you are drafting a peer-reviewed manuscript, a CDC surveillance report, or a grant application to NIH, precision and structure matter more than flourish. Good scientific writing is transparent about methods, honest about limitations, and organized so a busy reviewer finds the point fast. The standard IMRaD format (Introduction, Methods, Results, and Discussion) anchors most journal articles.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Define your audience and the single main message before writing a word.</li><li>Outline using IMRaD, mapping each result to a research question.</li><li>Draft the Methods first while procedures are fresh and reproducible.</li><li>Write in active voice with short sentences; cut jargon and redundancy.</li><li>Revise for accuracy, then edit for flow, and finally proofread citations.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An Emory epidemiologist drafts a manuscript on county vaccination coverage for the American Journal of Public Health, structuring it in IMRaD and following ICMJE authorship rules.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Publications and clear reports build your reputation and open doors to promotions, grants, and faculty positions.</p></div>",
 "Presenting Data and Findings": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Presenting Data and Findings</strong><br><span>Let the numbers tell a story</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-comm-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Presenting Data and Findings\"><figcaption>Presenting Data and Findings: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Audience</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Presenting Data and Findings.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Message</strong><span>Apply Presenting Data and Findings to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Action</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Presenting Data and Findings</summary><p>Presenting Data and Findings is the focus of this Communicating Like a Professional lesson. It develops the learner's ability to communicate public-health evidence clearly to technical and public audiences.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>key message</summary><p>A short, important point an audience should understand and remember.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>data visualization</summary><p>A chart, graph, or display used to communicate information.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>plain language</summary><p>Clear wording understandable to the intended audience.</p></details></section><p>Data only influences decisions when it is presented clearly. Public health professionals routinely translate surveillance figures, trial results, and program evaluations for varied audiences, from a state epidemiology team to a city council. The goal is not to show every number but to guide viewers to the insight that matters. Thoughtful chart choices, plain labels, and an honest scale keep your message credible and memorable.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify the one takeaway each figure should deliver.</li><li>Match the chart type to the data: trends use lines, comparisons use bars.</li><li>Label axes plainly, cite the source, and avoid misleading truncated scales.</li><li>Highlight the key value with color or annotation; mute everything else.</li><li>Rehearse a spoken narrative that walks the audience through the visual.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A analyst at a California health department presents opioid overdose trends to county commissioners using a single annotated line chart and a plain-language summary slide.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Leaders fund what they understand; clear data presentation makes you the person decision-makers trust.</p></div>",
 "Writing Policy Briefs": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Writing Policy Briefs</strong><br><span>Turn evidence into action</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-comm-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Writing Policy Briefs\"><figcaption>Writing Policy Briefs: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Audience</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Writing Policy Briefs.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Message</strong><span>Apply Writing Policy Briefs to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Action</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Writing Policy Briefs</summary><p>Writing Policy Briefs is the focus of this Communicating Like a Professional lesson. It develops the learner's ability to communicate public-health evidence clearly to technical and public audiences.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>key message</summary><p>A short, important point an audience should understand and remember.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>data visualization</summary><p>A chart, graph, or display used to communicate information.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>plain language</summary><p>Clear wording understandable to the intended audience.</p></details></section><p>A policy brief compresses research into a short, decision-ready document for legislators, agency directors, and other non-technical readers. Unlike a journal article, it leads with the recommendation and keeps methods in the background. Most briefs run one to four pages and follow a predictable arc: the problem, the evidence, the options, and a clear recommended action. Busy officials often read only the summary box, so every word earns its place.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>State the problem and its policy relevance in the opening paragraph.</li><li>Summarize the strongest evidence in plain language, not statistical detail.</li><li>Lay out two or three realistic policy options with trade-offs.</li><li>Give a clear, feasible recommendation tied to the decision-maker's authority.</li><li>Add a scannable summary box, headings, and a short reference list.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A public health advocate writes a two-page brief urging a state legislature to fund smoke-free housing, opening with a recommendation box and three costed options.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Briefs are how research shapes law and budgets, making this a high-leverage skill for policy careers.</p></div>",
 "Public Speaking and Presentations": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Public Speaking and Presentations</strong><br><span>Speak so people remember</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-comm-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Public Speaking and Presentations\"><figcaption>Public Speaking and Presentations: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Audience</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Public Speaking and Presentations.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Message</strong><span>Apply Public Speaking and Presentations to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Action</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Public Speaking and Presentations</summary><p>Public Speaking and Presentations is the focus of this Communicating Like a Professional lesson. It develops the learner's ability to communicate public-health evidence clearly to technical and public audiences.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>key message</summary><p>A short, important point an audience should understand and remember.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>data visualization</summary><p>A chart, graph, or display used to communicate information.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>plain language</summary><p>Clear wording understandable to the intended audience.</p></details></section><p>Public health work regularly puts you in front of an audience, whether briefing a health commissioner, teaching a community class, or presenting at a national conference. Strong speakers are not born; they prepare deliberately and practice. Confidence grows from knowing your material, structuring a clear arc, and managing nerves with rehearsal and breathing. A memorable talk delivers one core message the audience can repeat afterward.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Define your single core message and the action you want from listeners.</li><li>Build a clear opening hook, three main points, and a strong close.</li><li>Design slides that support you, using few words and clear visuals.</li><li>Rehearse aloud, ideally recorded or before a colleague, and time yourself.</li><li>Manage nerves with slow breathing, and invite questions confidently.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A doctoral student delivers a ten-minute oral abstract at the APHA Annual Meeting in Washington, opening with a patient story and closing with one clear policy ask.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Visible, articulate speakers get invited to lead panels, projects, and teams.</p></div>",
 "Facilitating Meetings and Trainings": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Facilitating Meetings and Trainings</strong><br><span>Guide groups toward outcomes</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-comm-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Facilitating Meetings and Trainings\"><figcaption>Facilitating Meetings and Trainings: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Audience</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Facilitating Meetings and Trainings.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Message</strong><span>Apply Facilitating Meetings and Trainings to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Action</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Facilitating Meetings and Trainings</summary><p>Facilitating Meetings and Trainings is the focus of this Communicating Like a Professional lesson. It develops the learner's ability to communicate public-health evidence clearly to technical and public audiences.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>key message</summary><p>A short, important point an audience should understand and remember.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>data visualization</summary><p>A chart, graph, or display used to communicate information.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>plain language</summary><p>Clear wording understandable to the intended audience.</p></details></section><p>Facilitation is the skill of helping a group think and decide together productively. Public health professionals lead coalition meetings, staff huddles, and workforce trainings where the facilitator sets the tone, keeps time, and ensures every voice is heard. A well-run session has a clear purpose, an agenda, and defined next steps. Poor facilitation wastes staff time and stalls programs, so structure and neutrality are essential.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Send a purpose statement and agenda with time allocations in advance.</li><li>Open by stating goals, ground rules, and the decisions to be made.</li><li>Draw out quieter participants and manage dominant voices tactfully.</li><li>Capture decisions, owners, and deadlines visibly as you go.</li><li>Close with a summary of action items and confirm the follow-up.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A program manager at a county health department facilitates a quarterly tobacco-control coalition meeting, using a timed agenda and a shared action tracker to keep partners accountable.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Facilitation marks you as someone who can move teams and partnerships forward, a core leadership signal.</p></div>",
 "Working with the Media and the Public": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Working with the Media and the Public</strong><br><span>Be a trusted public voice</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-comm-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Working with the Media and the Public\"><figcaption>Working with the Media and the Public: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Audience</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Working with the Media and the Public.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Message</strong><span>Apply Working with the Media and the Public to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Action</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Working with the Media and the Public</summary><p>Working with the Media and the Public is the focus of this Communicating Like a Professional lesson. It develops the learner's ability to communicate public-health evidence clearly to technical and public audiences.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>key message</summary><p>A short, important point an audience should understand and remember.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>data visualization</summary><p>A chart, graph, or display used to communicate information.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>plain language</summary><p>Clear wording understandable to the intended audience.</p></details></section><p>During outbreaks and health campaigns, the public turns to trusted experts, and reporters need clear, timely sources. Effective media engagement protects the public from misinformation and builds an agency's credibility. Whether giving a television interview or posting on social media, you should lead with a simple key message, stay within your expertise, and never speculate. Preparation and honesty matter more than polish.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Prepare three short key messages and anticipate tough questions.</li><li>Use plain language, avoiding jargon, acronyms, and speculation.</li><li>Bridge back to your key message when questions drift off course.</li><li>Correct misinformation calmly with facts and a credible source.</li><li>Coordinate with your communications office and follow embargo rules.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A CDC spokesperson gives a local news interview during a measles cluster, repeating three vaccination messages and directing viewers to the state health department website.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Skilled communicators become the public face of their agencies, a fast path to influence and leadership.</p></div>",
 "Continuing Education and Certifications": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Continuing Education and Certifications</strong><br><span>Keep growing after graduation</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-advance-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Continuing Education and Certifications\"><figcaption>Continuing Education and Certifications: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reflect</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Continuing Education and Certifications.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Develop</strong><span>Apply Continuing Education and Certifications to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sustain</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Continuing Education and Certifications</summary><p>Continuing Education and Certifications is the focus of this Grow and Advance Your Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to grow responsibly through learning, leadership, ethics, and professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>continuing professional development</summary><p>Ongoing learning used to maintain and improve professional competence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mentorship</summary><p>A developmental relationship involving guidance, support, and learning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional ethics</summary><p>Principles guiding responsible conduct and decisions at work.</p></details></section><p>Public health knowledge evolves quickly, and employers reward professionals who keep learning. Continuing education keeps your skills current, while credentials signal verified expertise to hiring managers. In the United States, the Certified in Public Health (CPH) credential from the National Board of Public Health Examiners is a widely recognized marker, requiring ongoing continuing-education credits to maintain. Certifications, short courses, and conferences together build a career that compounds over time.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>CPH credential</strong> – the NBPHE Certified in Public Health exam, renewed via continuing education.</li><li><strong>Specialty certifications</strong> – such as CHES, CIC infection control, or biostatistics credentials.</li><li><strong>Continuing education units</strong> – required to keep many credentials and licenses active.</li><li><strong>MOOCs and conferences</strong> – Coursera, CDC TRAIN, and APHA sessions expand skills affordably.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An Ohio health-department analyst earns the CPH credential, then maintains it by completing CDC TRAIN modules and attending the APHA Annual Meeting each year.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Credentials and fresh skills make your resume stand out and justify raises and promotions.</p></div>",
 "Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad</strong><br><span>Plan your graduate journey</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-advance-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad\"><figcaption>Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reflect</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Develop</strong><span>Apply Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sustain</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad</summary><p>Applying for an MPH or PhD Abroad is the focus of this Grow and Advance Your Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to grow responsibly through learning, leadership, ethics, and professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>continuing professional development</summary><p>Ongoing learning used to maintain and improve professional competence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mentorship</summary><p>A developmental relationship involving guidance, support, and learning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional ethics</summary><p>Principles guiding responsible conduct and decisions at work.</p></details></section><p>Graduate study deepens expertise and expands your career options. Top United States schools of public health, including Johns Hopkins, Harvard Chan, and Emory, admit competitive applicants based on academic record, experience, and fit. The United Kingdom, Canada, and Australia offer strong one-year masters and funded PhD routes as well. Applications reward early planning, especially for funding through assistantships, fellowships, or programs like Fulbright.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Research programs and faculty whose focus matches your goals.</li><li>Prepare academic transcripts, a strong GPA record, and any required GRE scores.</li><li>Write a focused statement of purpose linking your experience to that program.</li><li>Secure three strong recommendation letters from professors or supervisors.</li><li>Apply early for assistantships, fellowships, and Fulbright or school funding.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An applicant targets the Johns Hopkins MPH, tailoring a statement of purpose to a specific epidemiology track while also applying to funded programs in Canada and the UK.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>An advanced degree from a respected program accelerates access to leadership, research, and faculty roles.</p></div>",
 "Leadership and Management Skills": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Leadership and Management Skills</strong><br><span>Move from doing to leading</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-advance-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Leadership and Management Skills\"><figcaption>Leadership and Management Skills: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reflect</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Leadership and Management Skills.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Develop</strong><span>Apply Leadership and Management Skills to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sustain</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Leadership and Management Skills</summary><p>Leadership and Management Skills is the focus of this Grow and Advance Your Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to grow responsibly through learning, leadership, ethics, and professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>continuing professional development</summary><p>Ongoing learning used to maintain and improve professional competence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mentorship</summary><p>A developmental relationship involving guidance, support, and learning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional ethics</summary><p>Principles guiding responsible conduct and decisions at work.</p></details></section><p>As careers advance, technical skill matters less than the ability to lead people and manage resources. Leadership is about setting direction and inspiring others; management is about organizing work, budgets, and timelines to deliver results. Public health leaders run programs, supervise teams, and steward grant funds under real accountability. Both skill sets can be learned deliberately through practice, feedback, and reflection.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Vision</strong> – articulating a clear direction that motivates a team.</li><li><strong>Delegation</strong> – assigning work to develop staff, not just offload tasks.</li><li><strong>Budget stewardship</strong> – managing grant funds and reporting responsibly to funders.</li><li><strong>Emotional intelligence</strong> – reading people and handling conflict constructively.</li><li><strong>Accountability</strong> – setting measurable goals and owning outcomes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A new division director at a state health department manages a federally funded immunization grant, setting quarterly targets and coaching supervisors rather than doing the fieldwork herself.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Leadership ability determines whether you plateau as a specialist or rise to direct programs and agencies.</p></div>",
 "Professional Ethics and Integrity": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Professional Ethics and Integrity</strong><br><span>Trust is your foundation</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-advance-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Professional Ethics and Integrity\"><figcaption>Professional Ethics and Integrity: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reflect</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Professional Ethics and Integrity.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Develop</strong><span>Apply Professional Ethics and Integrity to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sustain</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Professional Ethics and Integrity</summary><p>Professional Ethics and Integrity is the focus of this Grow and Advance Your Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to grow responsibly through learning, leadership, ethics, and professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>continuing professional development</summary><p>Ongoing learning used to maintain and improve professional competence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mentorship</summary><p>A developmental relationship involving guidance, support, and learning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional ethics</summary><p>Principles guiding responsible conduct and decisions at work.</p></details></section><p>Public health rests on public trust, and that trust depends on ethical conduct. Professionals routinely handle sensitive data, allocate scarce resources, and make decisions affecting whole communities. Ethical practice means protecting confidentiality, disclosing conflicts of interest, and being honest even when the truth is inconvenient. Established frameworks, such as the American Public Health Association code of ethics, guide difficult judgments.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Confidentiality</strong> – protecting personal health data under HIPAA and agency policy.</li><li><strong>Conflict of interest</strong> – disclosing financial or personal ties that could bias decisions.</li><li><strong>Research integrity</strong> – honest data handling and proper IRB oversight of human subjects.</li><li><strong>Equity</strong> – distributing resources fairly and prioritizing vulnerable populations.</li><li><strong>Accountability</strong> – reporting errors and misconduct rather than concealing them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An epidemiologist recuses herself from a vaccine review after disclosing prior consulting income, and routes patient records through a HIPAA-compliant system.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A single ethical lapse can end a career, while a reputation for integrity earns lasting professional trust.</p></div>",
 "Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor</strong><br><span>Grow with, and through, others</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-advance-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor\"><figcaption>Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reflect</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Develop</strong><span>Apply Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sustain</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor</summary><p>Mentorship: Finding and Being a Mentor is the focus of this Grow and Advance Your Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to grow responsibly through learning, leadership, ethics, and professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>continuing professional development</summary><p>Ongoing learning used to maintain and improve professional competence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mentorship</summary><p>A developmental relationship involving guidance, support, and learning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional ethics</summary><p>Principles guiding responsible conduct and decisions at work.</p></details></section><p>Mentorship accelerates careers on both sides of the relationship. A good mentor offers guidance, honest feedback, and access to networks, while mentoring others sharpens your own leadership and reputation. Effective mentoring is intentional, with clear expectations and regular contact, not occasional hallway advice. Early-career professionals should seek mentors actively rather than waiting to be chosen.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Identify what you want to learn, then find mentors whose paths reflect it.</li><li>Reach out with a specific, respectful request for their time.</li><li>Set expectations together: frequency, goals, and how to prepare.</li><li>Come to each meeting with questions and updates on prior advice.</li><li>As you grow, offer mentorship to juniors and give feedback generously.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A CDC Epidemic Intelligence Service officer meets monthly with a senior epidemiologist for career guidance, then begins mentoring a summer intern at the county health department.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Mentored professionals advance faster, and being a mentor builds the visibility that leadership roles require.</p></div>",
 "Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout</strong><br><span>Sustain a long career</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-advance-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout\"><figcaption>Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reflect</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Develop</strong><span>Apply Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sustain</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout</summary><p>Work-Life Balance and Avoiding Burnout is the focus of this Grow and Advance Your Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to grow responsibly through learning, leadership, ethics, and professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>continuing professional development</summary><p>Ongoing learning used to maintain and improve professional competence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mentorship</summary><p>A developmental relationship involving guidance, support, and learning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional ethics</summary><p>Principles guiding responsible conduct and decisions at work.</p></details></section><p>Public health work is demanding, and outbreaks, deadlines, and emotional strain can lead to burnout, a state of exhaustion and reduced effectiveness. Protecting your health is not indulgence; it sustains the quality and longevity of your work. Balance comes from boundaries, realistic workloads, and support systems. Recognizing early warning signs lets you act before exhaustion becomes chronic.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Burnout signs</strong> – cynicism, exhaustion, and dropping performance are early warnings.</li><li><strong>Boundaries</strong> – protecting time off and disconnecting from email after hours.</li><li><strong>Workload management</strong> – prioritizing tasks and saying no when overcommitted.</li><li><strong>Support systems</strong> – colleagues, supervisors, and employee assistance programs.</li><li><strong>Self-care basics</strong> – sleep, exercise, and time with family and friends.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">After a demanding COVID-19 response, an analyst at a state health department negotiates flexible hours and uses the agency employee assistance program to recover before returning to full pace.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Sustainable habits keep you effective and present for the decades a public health career can span.</p></div>",
 "Building a Lasting Professional Network": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Building a Lasting Professional Network</strong><br><span>Relationships open doors</span></div><figure class=\"cl-lesson-visual ph-visual ph-advance-visual\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Public health career guide for Building a Lasting Professional Network\"><figcaption>Building a Lasting Professional Network: understand, apply and demonstrate</figcaption><div class=\"cl-visual-grid\"><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Reflect</strong><span>Understand the essential purpose and professional standards of Building a Lasting Professional Network.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Develop</strong><span>Apply Building a Lasting Professional Network to a realistic Liberian, NGO, government, or international public-health setting.</span></div><div class=\"cl-visual-item\"><strong>Sustain</strong><span>Document evidence, review the result, and identify the next improvement.</span></div></div></figure><section class=\"lesson-keywords\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h2>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h2><p>Tap a word to open its definition.</p><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>Building a Lasting Professional Network</summary><p>Building a Lasting Professional Network is the focus of this Grow and Advance Your Career lesson. It develops the learner's ability to grow responsibly through learning, leadership, ethics, and professional relationships.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>continuing professional development</summary><p>Ongoing learning used to maintain and improve professional competence.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>mentorship</summary><p>A developmental relationship involving guidance, support, and learning.</p></details><details class=\"lesson-keyword\"><summary>professional ethics</summary><p>Principles guiding responsible conduct and decisions at work.</p></details></section><p>Many opportunities in public health come through relationships rather than job boards. A strong network gives you access to advice, collaborators, and openings before they are advertised. Networking is not transactional self-promotion; it is building genuine, reciprocal relationships over time. Professionals who invest steadily in connections, both online and in person, find their careers full of options.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Steps</h4><ol><li>Build a complete LinkedIn profile and connect after meetings and events.</li><li>Join professional bodies such as APHA and attend their conferences.</li><li>Follow up after meeting someone with a short, specific message.</li><li>Offer help and share useful information before asking for favors.</li><li>Stay in touch periodically, not only when you need something.</li></ol></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A recent MPH graduate joins APHA, attends the Annual Meeting, and follows up with two presenters on LinkedIn, one of whom later refers her to a CDC fellowship opening.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A living network is often the single biggest driver of new jobs, grants, and collaborations across a career.</p></div>"
};
